Today's Study:
3.5 hr - Live lecture: Logging, tracing and ELK stack (hands-on with Terraform + Ansible)
         - Monitoring vs logging: monitoring shows that an incident happened,
           logs show why it happened
         - Metrics vs logs vs traces (interview question)
         - Trace ID and span: one ID follows a request across services
           (API gateway -> order -> payment -> bank), span = one operation
         - Tracing tools: OpenTelemetry, Jaeger, Zipkin, Tempo,
           Datadog APM, AWS X-Ray
         - Trace ID works at application layer (L7), traceroute works at
           network layer (L3), OSI layers revision
         - Real world monitoring: failed pod count, IP exhaustion in subnet,
           EBS usage, warning vs critical thresholds
         - ELK flow: Filebeat -> Logstash -> Elasticsearch -> Kibana
         - Logstash pipeline: input (beats 5044), filter (grok, date, geoip),
           output (Elasticsearch 9200), log parsing, log levels
         - Elasticsearch: indices, JSON documents, shards and replicas
         - Index lifecycle policy: hot / warm / cold / delete phases,
           rollover by age or size, priority
         - Hands-on: Terraform created ELK server + 2 clients, Ansible roles
           (elasticsearch, logstash, kibana, filebeat), yum repo + GPG key,
           checked services with systemctl status, top, free -h
         - Troubleshooting: private IP not reachable from local machine
           (used public IP), ansible-playbook --limit client2
         - Security group ports: 5601 (Kibana), 9200 (Elasticsearch),
           5044 (Logstash); logs stopped when ports were closed
         - Kibana: data view pathnex-logs*, Discover, dashboards with Lens
           (bar, metric, formula e.g. login success rate), duplicate panel,
           index management, alerting

